$ yo fix --help
Usage: yo fix <file-or-dir>... [options]

Apply the mechanical repairs the compiler already computed. A repair is
attached to a diagnostic only when exactly ONE edit fixes it, so 'fix' never
guesses between alternatives; '--error-format json' carries the same repair
as a structured field. Evaluation stops at the first error in a file, so fix
re-runs until a pass changes nothing (at most 10 passes), formatting each
file it rewrites. '--migrate' runs one of the migrations listed below instead;
a migration that was retired is no longer a mode, and the last release that
ships each is listed in docs/en-US/ERROR_DIAGNOSTICS.md.

Options:
  --exclude <path>          Exclude a directory/file (repeatable)
  --dry-run                 Print the repairs without writing
  --migrate deref           Run the deref migration instead of the
                            repairs: r -> r.* where a reference is read
                            or written whole, &r -> &r.*, and
                            v.* -> v.*.* where v.* was the Box/Rc deref
                            through a reference
  --migrate unsized         Run decision 49's migration: a lend &b of a
                            Box(Dyn(T)) where a &Dyn(T) is taken -> &b.*;
                            dyn(h) over a named handle is listed. Older
                            code steps through the release that last
                            accepts its spelling: v0.2.77 for the bare
                            Dyn(...) types, v0.2.78 for the plain dyn(v)
                            (yo version install 0.2.78), then this one
  --target <triple>         Evaluate for this target, as compile --target
                            does, so the target's platform arms are
                            repaired or migrated (default: the host)
  --std-path <dir>          Override the std library directory

Examples:
  $ yo fix ./src
  $ yo fix main.yo --dry-run
  $ yo fix ./std ./tests --migrate deref
